WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.085 --> 00:00:02.210
IRFAN BAQUI: Hey, everyone.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.300
Welcome to "Apigee Up Close."

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.500
My name is Irfan Baqui.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.630
I'm a customer engineer
with Google Cloud,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:11.980
helping our customers make
better architectural decisions,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:14.680
as well as better API
management decisions.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.390
And today we'll be talking about
insights driven APIs programs.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:23.080
And the objective is just
to share our philosophy

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.060
on analytics and monitoring
and relate it to your business.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.670
I'll give you a lot of examples
of how our customers are

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.480
leveraging insights to make
their businesses better,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.470
to make their API
programs better.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.140
And then toward the
end, I'll take you

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:42.190
through a demo that will
showcase everything that we

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.310
talk about in the presentation.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:49.450
But the central idea really is
that a business needs real time

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.300
insights on multiple
different levels to be agile.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.700
And I'll show you today on how
you can get those insights.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.170
So let's dive right into it.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.420
I'm sure you're
familiar with Apigee.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.870
It sits in the middle
of all of your services

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.070
and your applications.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.470
And all of the data
that goes through

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.350
between your applications
and your services,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.760
it goes through Apigee.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.520
And the good thing about
this is that Apigee

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.770
has a lot of data
passing through,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.080
and it can give you
the right insights

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.940
on how your services are
doing, both on a business level

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.540
as well as on an
operational level.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.010
And when we are talking
about this data,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.220
the one thing to keep in mind
is that the data is usually

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.818
associated with a
particular entity.

00:01:39.818 --> 00:01:41.360
So on the left you
have the customer.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.380
On the right you have
your back end systems.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.860
And that's how the data
flows, left to right and right

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:46.670
to left.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.170
And it is associated with
a customer, an application,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.550
and a developer.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.930
And it accesses
particular APIs, which

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.690
are part of different
products of yours.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.180
And in turn, those APIs--

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:07.550
in Apigee, then, they interact
with specific back end systems.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.050
So all of these
insights are very

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.280
specific to the
particular entity.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:14.750
Thus they can give
you a good glean

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.820
on how your business and
your API program is doing.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.400
So now let me tell you about
our philosophy behind insights.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.370
We look at insights in a
lot of different levels,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.160
and the first level is
just your core business.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.510
Everything starts at
your core business KPIs.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.960
What is your business strategy,
and what are the metrics

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.880
that you need to
track to achieve it?

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.400
And you would want
to track that.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.920
And you can do it in
Apigee in very close

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.750
to a real-time basis.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.050
Be it your revenue,
or your subscriptions,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.050
or the health of your
marketing campaigns,

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.020
you can track all of
those insights in Apigee.

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.570
The second one is the insights
about your API program.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:00.320
How is your API program doing?

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.260
How many developers are signing
up in a particular week?

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.050
The different applications
the developers are making,

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.730
how are they
consuming your APIs?

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:11.600
What are the experiences that
the end users are getting?

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.170
So all of those insights you can
get from a good API management

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.460
platform like Apigee.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.950
The next one is at
a product level.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.660
So within your API
program, you would

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.990
be exposing a lot of different
APIs bundled together

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:28.100
as a product that represent
a cohesive functionality,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.200
and you want to see how
those products are being

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.180
consumed by your customers.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.870
And you want to see how one
product relates to the other

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:38.720
and figure out how you
can make them better,

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:42.470
invest the right resources
to make them better.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.170
And lastly, the
operational level

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.450
analytics for the
operations teams.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:47.930
And these have to be real-time.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.710
So if there is something
wrong in your APIs,

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.650
on Apigee or even in
your back end systems,

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.680
you want to get
alerted in real time

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:01.180
with the right amount of
information that will help you

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.130
solve that particular error.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.100
And you'd be able to
get that with Apigee.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.830
So now let me talk about
some of our customers that

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:12.260
are using Apigee Analytics on
all of these different levels.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.860
So one of the customers I want
to talk about is T-Mobile.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:18.970
T-Mobile is one of the largest
telecommunications providers

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.780
in the United States, and they
are reinventing themselves

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.490
as a technology company.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.990
They are modernizing
their entire stack,

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.380
and they're using
Apigee as the API layer

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.430
on top of everything to manage
the APIs, and govern them,

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:34.430
and all of that.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.780
So one of their key business
metrics that they track

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.760
are subscriptions.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.090
And they're able to
get business insights

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:47.620
into how the API program is
supporting that key business

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.500
strategy and how many
subscriptions they're driving.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.170
So the thing about T-Mobile,
it has a good online presence.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:57.010
It also has a very
good brick-and-mortar,

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.890
concrete, offline presence.

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:03.280
It has thousands of
stores across the US,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:05.410
but at the same time,
they're also selling

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:07.570
through big-box retailers.

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:14.290
And they would want to find
out what kinds of demographics

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:18.910
resonate better in one
channel versus the other.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.600
The people who would buy
T-Mobile subscriptions

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.160
in the retailer Target may
be of a different demographic

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.380
than folks that buy
the subscriptions

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.960
at Best Buy or Walmart.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.470
And you want to make sure that
you are appealing to them,

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:38.320
you're providing the right kind
of product to each one of them.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.560
And that's possible by
gaining the right insights

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:42.970
from that demographic.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.780
The other customer I want to
talk about is Domino's Pizza.

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:49.120
Now, you would think that
Domino's is in the pizza

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.010
delivery business,
but actually they

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:55.150
are in a B2B business servicing
their franchise owners.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:06:02.080
And these franchisees, they have
their own IT systems, usually,

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.290
and then Domino's needs
a way to integrate

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:08.860
into their delivery systems
and their payment systems

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:09.550
and all of that.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:12.460
And they are able to
do that with Apigee.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:17.860
But at the same time, to
stay competitive and lure

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:22.540
the franchise owners in staying
with Domino's and opening up

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:26.470
even more franchises,
they need to provide them

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:28.690
with relevant
insights on how they

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.030
can make their programs better.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:34.390
And with a platform like
Apigee, as all of the data

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:38.440
is flowing through, they would
be able to give them insights

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:44.740
on, what does the best
delivery experience look like?

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:46.810
Who are the target
demographics, what

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:50.590
are they interested in
across different geographies?

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.250
And what are the success
factors and success criterias

00:06:54.250 --> 00:06:57.670
that the franchise owners
can implement to make

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.440
their businesses better?

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:02.350
The other customer I want to
talk to you about is Walgreens.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.840
And Walgreens is really
a classic example

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:11.380
of a customer that has developed
a robust developer ecosystem.

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:15.370
Walgreens, if you're aware, it
is one of the largest pharmacy

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.380
chains in the United States.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:23.110
And back in 2008, they
started to experience a drop

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:25.900
in their photo business because
folks weren't using film

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:29.590
roll anymore, and they were
using their smartphones instead

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:30.890
to take pictures.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.790
And then it was really
important for them

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:36.430
to understand the
end user behaviors

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.110
and figure out how to
get those users back.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.720
Well, they found out that
they were taking pictures

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.120
with their smartphones,
but they still

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.420
wanted to get those
pictures printed.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.630
So they created some native
applications, mobile as well as

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:55.200
desktop, to
basically enable them

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.710
to get those photos printed
at a nearby Walgreens.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:01.360
And that restored some
of their business,

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.540
but they said,
hey, I think there

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.560
is some more potential here.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:10.470
And they started to get insights
into what kinds of pictures

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.290
were these customers printing.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:16.140
And they found out that
a lot of this traffic

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.600
was coming from other photo
services that would enhance

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.840
their customers' photos.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:25.740
For example, at
Halloween time you

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:28.950
would upload a picture
to one of these services

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.980
and decorate it with
pumpkins or something.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.740
So they were experiencing a lot
of traffic from such services.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:40.740
They said, all right, how
about I expose my photo prints

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:44.190
API to these different
applications and services

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:49.080
and incentivize them
to route their traffic

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.380
to get the photos printed
at a nearby Walgreens store?

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.920
So they did that, and
their ecosystem exploded.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.460
There were actually new
apps that the developers

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:03.330
were making just to
tap into the incentives

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:07.500
that Walgreens would give
them to direct traffic

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:08.710
to their stores.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.830
So that was very
successful, and then

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:15.370
they replicated that model
to other products, as well.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:19.260
So now they have a plethora
of products ranging

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.890
from prescription refills,
and rewards programs, and home

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:27.180
delivery services that
are basically just

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:28.770
APIs that they're exposing.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:32.520
And they're being integrated
in the developer's applications

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:38.130
and thereby giving more business
and traffic to Walgreens.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.120
And they were able to do so by
really understanding their end

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.520
customers and their
shopping patterns,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.360
as well as understanding
developers, what they need,

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:53.087
and incentivizing them
to partner with them

00:09:53.087 --> 00:09:54.420
and send them the right traffic.

00:09:57.272 --> 00:09:58.730
The next thing I
want to talk about

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.950
is operational insights
and monitoring.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:03.638
And this is actually used
by a lot of our customers

00:10:03.638 --> 00:10:05.180
in a lot of different
verticals, so I

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.510
won't give you a particular
customer's example.

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:12.380
But let me tell you about
the motivation, actually,

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:19.910
of why we created the
Monitoring module in Apigee.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:25.700
So some of the largest retailers
in the US and across the globe,

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:28.550
actually, are
Apigee's customers.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.580
And at critical
times of the year,

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:35.900
like the New Year's sale or the
Black Friday or Cyber Monday

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:40.560
sale, they experience
enormous amount of traffic.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:45.200
So for example, in
2018, Black Friday,

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.210
we experienced close
to 110,000 transactions

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.870
per second sustained over
long durations of time.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:58.235
And it's important that these
services for these customers,

00:10:58.235 --> 00:11:01.160
they're up and running
without any disruption,

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.040
because every
second of disruption

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.870
could cost them
hundreds of thousands,

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.615
if not millions of
dollars in revenue.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:17.240
So Apigee has a really
robust API availability rate.

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.860
We surpass our SLAs.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.360
But our customers
said that, hey,

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.020
sometimes there are
things on our end

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.280
that we need to troubleshoot.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:29.090
Some of our services
get down for a variety

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:30.650
of different
reasons, and we would

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:34.970
love to get some
alerts for us to be

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.940
able to fix those
issues fast and get up

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:39.080
and running really fast.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.470
So that was the motivation
behind Apigee Monitoring.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:48.530
It addresses a particular
need for the operations teams.

00:11:48.530 --> 00:11:50.180
There's a lot of
pressure these days

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.810
for them to resolve issues very
fast, quickly diagnose issues,

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:57.080
and the data that
Apigee has, which

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:00.830
is specific to the
entities accessing the APIs

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:04.460
as well as services
that the API is tied to.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.760
And the different
policies in your proxies

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:11.450
and where exactly that
error might be occurring

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:16.680
is very useful in diagnosing
these APIs issues.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.300
So this is a diagram of how
Apigee Monitoring differs

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.990
from just generic
synthetic monitoring.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.170
The thing I want
you to focus on is

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:30.740
that the alerts that you get--

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:31.730
they are precise.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:35.360
They tell you exactly where
the error has occurred.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:37.280
They're contextual.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:40.730
If the error is due
to a race condition,

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:43.190
if it's intermittent,
it'll help you

00:12:43.190 --> 00:12:48.380
zero in on what exactly is
wrong and what is triggering

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:49.920
that particular error.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:53.330
So just to sum it up,
Apigee Analytics actually

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:59.240
covers all of the personas
that need insights into the API

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.000
program to make it better.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:04.310
It is your business
owner to get insights

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.610
on the strategy
for the API program

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.760
and how it's doing with
relation to the different KPIs

00:13:10.760 --> 00:13:12.570
that the business team has set.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:16.100
It's to the API team can monitor
the health of the API program

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.580
and figure out what is
gaining traction, what

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:23.480
needs more resources; to the
app developer to gain insights

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:27.070
into particular API
proxies and applications;

00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:32.290
and to ops teams to get
real-time monitoring alerts

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:35.710
and help in
diagnosing the issues.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:40.180
So at this, let me
jump right into a demo.

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:43.190
So this is how the
Apigee platform looks.

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.880
You have a place to
design your APIs.

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.400
You have a place to
create, build your APIs,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:50.780
productize them, publish
them, and all of that.

00:13:50.780 --> 00:13:53.740
And then you have a
place for analytics.

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.500
And to the left of the
screen, as you can see,

00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:00.580
these are the out-of-the-box
dashboards that you have

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:05.660
to target specific personas
within your organization.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.400
I would like to
begin with developers

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:11.145
because once you
have an API program,

00:14:11.145 --> 00:14:12.520
usually the first
thing you think

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:16.600
about is, how are my
developers consuming

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:19.460
my APIs, what is my engagement
rate, and all of that.

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:23.970
So this dashboard will
give us just that.

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:27.690
So right now as
you can see, there

